## Suite speed

**This is a measurement procedure, not a lever list.** The two most-cited Django speed
levers (`--reuse-db`, `synchronous_commit=off`) measured at 1.9% and 5% in this fleet; the
lever almost no guide names first — the MD5 test password hasher — was the only one worth
a full factor, and even it turned out to be the *weakest* of three that compose. A
recommended-lever list is a claim about a project nobody measured (`m1`); this section
runs a procedure against the project under audit and records what it actually finds in the
results table below. Full incidents and figures:
`skills/aidex-coverage/references/02-best-practices.md` and
`research/2026-08-22-suite-speed-and-coverage-findings/` in the aidex repo's `.context/`.

### m1 — Measure the lever before recommending it

Run the project's suite once, unmodified, and record the wall time before touching any
configuration. Every figure below this line is a measured delta against that number, not
an assumed one. Example of the failure this guards against, so the illustration is not
itself a lever list: round one of the 2026-08-21/22 campaign concluded the MD5 hasher was
"the one real lever." Round two, measuring three candidates together for the first time,
found it the **weakest of the three that work** on the NS project — hasher 2.30x × xdist
n=8 2.99x × removing `debug_toolbar` 2.00x = **13.63x** compounded, taking that suite from
29:51 to 2:11. A rule against publishing lever lists whose own illustration turns out to
be wrong is the most followable version of this rule available — measure this project,
don't reuse another project's ranking.

### m2 — Profile the distribution before choosing a class of lever

Run with `--durations` (pytest) or the equivalent before choosing a lever. A flat
distribution (many tests at a similar small cost, no small set dominating the clock)
rules out "chase the slow tests" as a strategy — in one measured case the 120 slowest
tests were only 13.3% of the total clock. A flat distribution admits three lever classes
only: cut the fixed per-test cost, parallelise, or run fewer tests. A concentrated
distribution (a handful of tests dominating) makes all three over-engineering — fix those
tests instead. The profile costs one run; do it before picking a lever class.

### m3 — Never measure a parallel mode on cold state

Any mode with persistent state (xdist worker databases, caches, containers) must be
measured with a discarded warm-up run first, then at steady state — never on the first,
cold invocation. A cold-state measurement of xdist in this fleet showed "no good here";
the same lever at steady state, on the same suite, gave **2.99x** at 8 workers (263.0 s
against a clean 787.0 s interleaved sequential mean). The cold reading was an artifact of
the instrument, not the lever, and it closed a real door for two weeks before being
re-measured. State explicitly in the write-up that a warm-up was taken and discarded.

### m4 — Measure the invocation floor from the inside, never by difference

Measure what a single invocation costs (process start, framework setup, before any test
body runs) directly — instrument the floor itself — rather than inferring it as the
difference between two long runs, and never inherit a "typical" cost from a usage corpus.
A usage corpus in this fleet charged one Docker invocation path a 15 s median from
observed runs; measured directly the real difference between the two invocation paths
was **0.68–0.70 s**. The corpus number was cold-stack cost the corpus never labelled as
such, and the cheaper path could not even run in that cold state — so it never appears in
the corpus carrying its own true cost.

### m6 — Recommend xdist by suite duration, never by project or by `-n auto`

Measure the same parallel lever on this project's own suite duration before recommending
a worker count; do not assume a count that worked elsewhere transfers. The same lever
measured **2.99x on an ~800 s suite and only 1.30x on a 19 s one**, with 2 workers
*below* sequential in both cases. Worker startup and database creation amortise against
total work — a 19 s suite has nothing to amortise against.

**Ban `-n auto` outright**, for two reasons: it resolves to a different worker count on
every machine, so timings stop being comparable between developers or between CI runs; and
each worker costs one database connection plus a database to create, migrate and maintain.
Choose a fixed integer, by measuring — this project's own elbow, not a borrowed one. In one
fleet project, clean interleaved measurement put the elbow at: 1.85x at 4 workers, 2.51x at
6, 2.99x at 8, **still gaining at 8** with clearly diminishing marginal returns (1.85x →
1.35x → 1.19x marginal per step) — record this project's own curve rather than assuming it
flattens where another project's did.

`--dist loadfile` versus `--dist loadscope`: **do not carry a preference between these into
a recommendation.** A clean interleaved measurement at n=8 put them 1.7% apart (267.5 s vs
272.0 s) — inside noise. Report whichever mode the project already uses; there is nothing
here to change it for.

### m8 — In E2E, find out which fraction does not scale before assuming it is startup

Run the E2E suite **twice** with services already live, before touching worker count. If
the second run costs the same as the first, the flat share is not startup — go look at what
the workers share (a shared dev server transforming modules on demand is a common cause,
not a per-worker cost). One measured case attributed a non-scaling clock to startup and was
wrong: `globalSetup` cost ~17 s and a database template clone cost 112 ms, neither large
enough to explain it; the actual cause was every worker sharing one development server.

**State which quantity you mean, and show the division.** Two different numbers describe
"how much doesn't scale" and they are not interchangeable: the non-scaling *share of a
single-worker run* (fixed cost ÷ total time at n=1) and the *clock still present at N
workers relative to n=1* (time at N ÷ time at 1) answer different questions and can differ
by several points — in one measured case, 249/316 = 79% by the first definition and
275.0/315.9 = 87% by the second. Printing "88% does not scale" without saying which
division was meant, and without the arithmetic, is not a reproducible claim; this playbook
rejects it as a finding until both are shown.

### m10 — A matching test total is necessary, not sufficient: compare the work actually done

Before treating any before/after pair as comparable, check three things, not one:

1. **Same total test count** in both arms.
2. **Same work done** — passed-test count within about 2% between arms.
3. **No errors in either arm.**

A matching total alone can hide mass erroring: a fleet project measured `n_tests = 2684` in
both arms of a configuration change with an apparent 1.41x "improvement," while 1963 of
those tests were erroring at setup and doing no work in the faster arm. The total holds
while the clock falls — the perfect disguise for a false speed-up, and the reason clause 1
alone (the comparability rule this playbook already applies to load, `m5`, see
`skills/aidex-conventions/references/measurement-conventions.md`) is not enough on its own.
Whatever computes "total" in your tooling must sum **every** outcome category (passed,
failed, skipped, errored, xfailed) — a parser that drops one category can under-report the
total enough to hide exactly this failure.

